{
  "statement": "The number one killer of startups is not bad code. It is building something nobody wants. CB Insights analyzed over 100 failed startups and found that 42% died because there was no market need for their product. Not because the architecture was ugly. Not because the codebase was hard to maintain. Because they never got real answers from real users fast enough. For a low-risk consumer app with a 90-day shipping window, the existential threat is silence — zero users, zero feedback, zero revenue signal — while your runway burns down to nothing. Every week you spend polishing code that might get thrown away is a week you are not learning whether this product deserves to exist. That is not caution. That is waste.\n\nHere is the dirty secret every honest engineer knows: MVP code is throwaway code, whether a human wrote it or a large language model generated it. Stripe shipped a rough first version in weeks and rebuilt it after they proved the market wanted what they were selling. The difference with AI-generated code is speed. What used to take a small team two months now takes days. Early data from GitHub Copilot studies show that AI-assisted developers complete tasks up to 55% faster with comparable or lower defect density at the prototype stage. The \"knowledge void\" concern — that nobody fully understands AI-generated code — is real. But it is structurally identical to what happens when a founding engineer quits, which is every other startup story ever told. You solve it with automated test coverage and documentation tooling, not by slowing the whole operation to a crawl.\n\nSo let's stop arguing about whether AI-generated code is perfect and start measuring it. I am proposing three concrete, falsifiable tripwires. First: if user-facing bugs exceed a defined threshold per two-week sprint, you slow generation and increase human review. Second: if refactoring any AI-authored module after market validation takes longer than two sprint cycles, that module gets a full rewrite in Phase 2. Third: AI-generated code ships with automated tests covering critical user paths, and if test coverage drops below 70%, code generation pauses until coverage is restored. These are not vibes. These are measurable gates that let a team run fast while keeping a hand on the brake. This is \"move fast and measure everything,\" not \"move fast and break things.\"\n\nThe fight between Product, Engineering, and Finance dissolves the moment you ship. Product gets feature validation from actual users in weeks instead of guessing for months. Engineering gets real defect signals from deployment — the kind that code review alone never catches. Finance gets growth-per-dollar data they can actually model. A two-phase budget makes this concrete: Phase 1, the first 90 days, optimizes for deployment speed with a high percentage of AI-generated code. Phase 2, pre-committed before you write line one, allocates a fixed engineering budget for refactoring the modules that survived validation. Finance gets predictable costs. Engineering gets a guaranteed rewrite window. The tension between departments is not a values conflict — it is a sequencing problem, and speed solves it by giving everyone the data they need to converge.\n\nThe real question this debate has to answer is not \"what if the code is messy?\" It is \"what is the cost of not shipping?\" Every day of delay is a day with zero validated learning, zero user signal, and competitors filling the gap you left open. For a low-risk consumer product with no safety-critical features, the asymmetry is total: the downside of shipping rough code is a rewrite you already budgeted for. The downside of not shipping is death by irrelevance. Who wants to defend that trade?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🚀", "tooltip": "A rocket burns most of its fuel just to leave the ground — the MVP phase is about escaping the gravity of zero users, not about fuel efficiency."},
    {"symbol": "⏳", "tooltip": "Every grain of sand that falls is runway you cannot recover — delay is not free, it is the most expensive choice a startup can make."},
    {"symbol": "🔬", "tooltip": "A microscope focused on code quality when the real specimen is market truth — measure what matters, not what feels safe."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "CB Insights analyzed over 100 failed startups and found that 42% died because there was no market need for their product.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "Early data from GitHub Copilot studies show that AI-assisted developers complete tasks up to 55% faster with comparable or lower defect density at the prototype stage.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "If test coverage drops below 70%, code generation pauses until coverage is restored.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "The downside of shipping rough code is a rewrite you already budgeted for. The downside of not shipping is death by irrelevance.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "The tension between departments is not a values conflict — it is a sequencing problem, and speed solves it by giving everyone the data they need to converge.", "targets": []}
  ]
}

